{"id": "en_thin_ice_p2", "total": 76.39, "segments": [{"text": "Part two. Mark pulled himself out of freezing water, but his wet clothes freeze in subzero air.", "start": 0.0, "end": 4.96}, {"text": "He remembers the 1-10-1 rule and knows he has 60 minutes before hypothermia makes him pass out.", "start": 5.54, "end": 10.1}, {"text": "Crawling low across frozen ice keeps his weight spread out while moving toward the dark treeline.", "start": 10.8, "end": 14.246}, {"text": "Staying on his stomach prevents thin ice from cracking again under his heavy freezing body weight.", "start": 15.42, "end": 19.1}, {"text": "Once his knees hit firm ground at shore, he must act quickly to save his life.", "start": 19.62, "end": 22.86}, {"text": "One. Remove freezing wet clothing immediately after reaching safe shore shelter to stop rapid heat loss.", "start": 23.7, "end": 28.88}, {"text": "Trapped cold water against skin drains body temperature much faster than subzero air around your chest.", "start": 29.4, "end": 33.76}, {"text": "Two. Shield your body from blowing wind behind dense trees or big shore rocks right away.", "start": 34.54, "end": 38.74}, {"text": "Blocking freezing wind keeps precious remaining core heat inside your vital organs while waiting for help.", "start": 39.42, "end": 43.32}, {"text": "Three. Move your arms and legs constantly to generate internal muscle heat without sweating heavy drops.", "start": 44.12, "end": 48.76}, {"text": "Active movement keeps blood flowing to critical body parts during the long wait for rescue teams.", "start": 49.46, "end": 52.94}, {"text": "Mark stays sheltered in dense trees, keeping warm until emergency responders arrive on the lake shore.", "start": 53.8, "end": 57.58}, {"text": "By following every official cold water step, Mark survives extreme wilderness cold without passing out.", "start": 58.06, "end": 62.52}, {"text": "Cold water kills fast, but controlling panic and acting quickly gives you time to reach safety.", "start": 63.14, "end": 67.34}, {"text": "Remember the 1-10-1 rule whenever walking across unpredictable frozen ice surfaces. Follow for a new scenario tomorrow.", "start": 67.88, "end": 73.24}], "words": [["Part", 0.0, 0.26], ["two.", 0.246, 0.491], ["Mark", 1.24, 1.36], ["pulled", 1.36, 1.56], ["himself", 1.56, 1.82], ["out", 1.82, 2.06], ["of", 2.06, 2.16], ["freezing", 2.16, 2.44], ["water,", 2.44, 2.8], ["but", 3.08, 3.14], ["his", 3.14, 3.26], ["wet", 3.26, 3.38], ["clothes", 3.38, 3.76], ["freeze", 3.76, 4.12], ["in", 4.12, 4.38], ["subzero", 4.13, 4.469], ["air.", 4.72, 4.96], ["He", 5.54, 5.82], ["remembers", 5.82, 5.98], ["the", 5.98, 6.16], ["1-10-1", 5.99, 5.894], ["rule", 6.94, 7.2], ["and", 7.52, 7.58], ["knows", 7.58, 7.72], ["he", 7.72, 7.88], ["has", 7.88, 7.98], ["60", 7.98, 8.32], ["minutes", 8.32, 8.54], ["before", 8.54, 8.82], ["hypothermia", 8.82, 9.38], ["makes", 9.38, 9.54], ["him", 9.54, 9.7], ["pass", 9.7, 9.86], ["out.", 9.86, 10.1], ["Crawling", 10.8, 11.08], ["low", 11.08, 11.28], ["across", 11.28, 11.58], ["frozen", 11.58, 11.94], ["ice", 11.94, 12.08], ["keeps", 12.08, 12.4], ["his", 12.4, 12.56], ["weight", 12.56, 12.7], ["spread", 12.7, 13.04], ["out", 13.04, 13.22], ["while", 13.22, 13.36], ["moving", 13.36, 13.64], ["toward", 13.64, 13.94], ["the", 13.94, 14.12], ["dark", 14.12, 14.3], ["treeline.", 14.129999999999999, 14.246], ["Staying", 15.42, 15.74], ["on", 15.43, 14.786], ["his", 15.44, 14.983], ["stomach", 15.84, 16.06], ["prevents", 16.06, 16.38], ["thin", 16.38, 16.74], ["ice", 16.74, 16.94], ["from", 16.94, 17.16], ["cracking", 17.16, 17.42], ["again", 17.42, 17.74], ["under", 17.74, 17.98], ["his", 17.98, 18.1], ["heavy", 18.1, 18.28], ["freezing", 18.28, 18.7], ["body", 18.7, 18.92], ["weight.", 18.92, 19.1], ["Once", 19.62, 19.92], ["his", 19.92, 20.08], ["knees", 20.08, 20.22], ["hit", 20.22, 20.46], ["firm", 20.46, 20.68], ["ground", 20.68, 20.88], ["at", 20.88, 21.02], ["shore,", 21.02, 21.18], ["he", 21.58, 21.64], ["mus